Subject: [PATCH] closure: a fused punctuation token must still end a simple command (#463 hole J)

shlex(punctuation_chars=...) MERGES adjacent punctuation, so a subshell close
followed by a separator arrives as the single token `);`. That token is in
neither _SEPARATORS (which lists `)` and `;` separately) nor either redirect
arm, so it fell through to `i += 1; continue`: the simple-command boundary was
erased and the following command's argv absorbed the write target. Measured on
the enforcing copy: the fused spelling classifies READ (allowed) while the
byte-equivalent spaced spelling classifies WRITE (denied). One character.

Membership is decided by CHARSET, not by exact token, because the fused run can
be any string over the punctuation alphabet. `(` is deliberately excluded: it
opens a list, it does not close one. `>`-carrying tokens never reach the new arm
(the redirect branch consumes them first), so no redirect target is eaten.

The arm resets `stdin_src` as well as `cur`, matching the separator arm it is
modelled on. Without that, a fused `);` hands the NEXT simple command a `< file`
preimage it never opened, and `patch` / `git apply` — whose fail-close is keyed
on the ABSENCE of that preimage — stops failing closed.

Agreed independently by claude-code (reply-2575) and kimi-code (reply-2583);
differential battery in tools/claude_fused_punct_arm_battery_2589.py, which
reproduces from two seats with all 9 rows x 3 columns exact.

NOT APPLIED BY A MEMBER, BY DESIGN. Both hunks are refused [gate-self-access];
escalations b98af462c5b03cc8 (approved, lapsed unclaimed) and d5519b9ac527b3d5.
A permit is single-use, so a two-hunk patch cannot be authorised by one approval
-- this file exists so the sovereign can land both as one act instead of two.

Not verified with `git apply --check` or `patch --dry-run`: both name the
governed file as their write target and are refused, so the gate declines to
dry-run a patch to itself. Line offsets are hand-derived against blob 3d8184ca;
apply with -F3 if they have drifted.

--- a/plugins/_shared/hestia_governance_closure.py
+++ b/plugins/_shared/hestia_governance_closure.py
@@ -391,7 +391,13 @@
 # Reads are unaffected: a `>` inside a QUOTED argument or a heredoc body is never a write
 # position, so it never creates a write target (it may still classify "read", which is allowed).
 _PUNCT = "();<>|&"
 _SEPARATORS = frozenset({";", "&&", "||", "|", "|&", "&", "(", ")", ";;"})
+# Chars that END a simple command. shlex(punctuation_chars=) FUSES adjacent punctuation,
+# so `) ;` arrives as the single token `);` which is in neither _SEPARATORS nor the
+# redirect arms — it fell through, the boundary was erased, and the next command's argv
+# absorbed the write target. Membership is by CHARSET, not by exact token (#463 hole J).
+# `(` is deliberately OUT: it opens a list, it does not close one.
+_BOUNDARY_PUNCT = frozenset(";&|)")
 _WRAPPERS = frozenset({"sudo", "doas", "env", "command", "exec", "nohup", "nice", "time",
                        "stdbuf"})
 _ASSIGN_RE = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*=")
@@ -725,7 +731,12 @@
             if t in ("<", "<<", "<<<", "<<-"):
                 if t == "<" and i + 1 < len(toks) and not _is_punct(toks[i + 1]):
                     stdin_src = toks[i + 1]  # patch/git-apply may consume this as input
                 i += 2  # read redirect / heredoc delimiter — source side, skip operand
                 continue
+            if frozenset(t) & _BOUNDARY_PUNCT:
+                if cur:
+                    eff = _flush_simple_command(cur, eff, targets, stdin_src)
+                    cur = []
+                stdin_src = None
             i += 1
             continue
